{"name":"Grapefruit Pulp","hex":"#fe6f5e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grapefruit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The tree of the species Citrus paradisi, a hybrid of Citrus maxima and sweet orange.","The large spherical tart fruit produced by this tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grapefruit"},{"term":"pulp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft, moist, shapeless mass or matter.\nA mixture of wood, cellulose and/or rags and water ground up to make paper.\nA mass of chemically processed wood fibres (cellulose).\nA suspension of mineral particles, typically achieved by some form of agitation.\nThe soft center of a fruit.","A mixture of wood, cellulose and/or rags and water ground up to make paper."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or be made into pulp.","To beat to a pulp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pulp"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grapefruit","blocks":[{"text":"The grapefruit is a subtropical citrus tree known for its relatively large, sour to semi-sweet, somewhat bitter fruit. The flesh of the fruit is segmented and varies in color from pale yellow to dark red."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Grapefruits_-_whole-halved-segments.jpg/330px-Grapefruits_-_whole-halved-segments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grapefruit","wikidata":"Q41350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg/500px-Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg/1280px-Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg/500px-Grapefruit.ebola.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikip